# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Franchise backend — module-local configuration
#
# ConfigModule loads this file first, then falls back to apps/api/.env for the
# ~120 platform-wide values (Redis, Kafka brokers, JWT secret, region config)
# that every service shares. Only variables this module owns belong here.
#
# Copy to `.env` and adjust. Anything set here overrides the platform file.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The documented first run (`npm run infra:up`) starts ONE Postgres, and each
# module owns a schema inside kartseek_db. These values point there — they are
# the live default because they are what the documented setup actually gives
# you. The file used to default to the dedicated instance below, which
# `npm run infra:up` does not start, so a fresh clone's first boot failed with
# ECONNREFUSED on a port nothing was listening on (AUD2-021).
#
# `docker compose --profile isolated up -d` gives this module its own Postgres
# on 5440; if you use it, uncomment the block at the end of this section.
# Ports: 5433 marketplace, 5434 grocery, 5435 restaurant, 5436 pharmacy,
# 5437 doctor, 5438 hotel, 5439 taxi, 5440 franchise.
#
# The schema inside is always `franchise` — fixed in the module, not configurable —
# so the same entity definitions work against either topology.
#
# FRANCHISE_DB_USER is the module's own login role, not the superuser.
# `infra/postgres/init-roles.sh` creates it with rights to the `franchise` schema
# and nothing else, so a compromise of this service is not a compromise of the
# other seven or of `public.users` (AUD2-073). Its password is the same
# FRANCHISE_DB_PASSWORD the root `.env` defines for the dedicated instance:
# one credential per module, valid in either topology.
FRANCHISE_DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
FRANCHISE_DB_PORT=5432
FRANCHISE_DB_NAME=kartseek_db
FRANCHISE_DB_USER=franchise_user

# No default, and none anywhere in the platform: `databaseCredentials()` throws
# if this is unset rather than falling back to a password published in tracked
# source. Copy the value from the repository-root `.env`.
FRANCHISE_DB_PASSWORD=

# Dedicated instance (`docker compose --profile isolated up -d`):
# FRANCHISE_DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
# FRANCHISE_DB_PORT=5440
# FRANCHISE_DB_NAME=kartseek_franchise

# ── Schema source ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off, and refused if you turn it on. Two guards, no dev escape hatch:
#   • validateDatabaseConfig() in main.ts throws on DB_SYNCHRONIZE=true in EVERY
#     environment, this one included;
#   • assertSynchronizeAllowed() in the TypeORM factory throws if auto-sync
#     reaches it under NODE_ENV=production (reachable when SKIP_DB=true has
#     skipped the first guard).
#
# The schema comes from migrations/ and nothing else:
#
#     cd modules/franchise/backend && npm run migration:show && npm run migration:run
#
# To iterate on entities, generate a migration against a scratch database —
# docs/guides/database-migrations.md, "Generating a migration". Auto-sync is not
# an option there either: it rewrites the schema from the entity definitions on
# every boot, and annotating an existing column is enough to make it DROP and
# recreate that column, which empties it.
DB_SYNCHRONIZE=false

# ── Transport ports ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Kept identical to the values the API gateway's client registrations default
# to. Changing one here means changing FRANCHISE_TCP_PORT
# on the gateway side too, or calls will silently go to the wrong place.
FRANCHISE_SERVICE_PORT=3016
FRANCHISE_TCP_PORT=4006
